P2.11: shared subprocess runner on BaseScanner + per-item guards

Every scanner rolled its own `subprocess.run(..., capture_output=True,
text=True, timeout=.., check=False)` wrapped in an ad-hoc try/except, with
inconsistent error handling. Added BaseScanner.run_cmd() as the one safe
runner: returns the CompletedProcess or None on any failure (missing binary /
timeout / OSError), and records an error only when given a `label` (so an
optional-tool-absent stays silent while a real failure surfaces as
partial_success).

- systemd: all five subprocess sites now go through run_cmd; labelled the
  list-units/list-unit-files calls, left show/is-enabled silent (best-effort).
- certs: dropped the local _run helper for run_cmd; sudo fallbacks unchanged.

Per-item resilience: a single malformed systemd unit or cert lineage used to
abort the whole scanner (losing every other asset as the exception bubbled to
execute()). Both build loops now wrap each item in try/except -> add_error +
continue, so one bad item degrades to partial_success instead of zero assets.

cron's `crontab -l` keeps its bespoke not-installed-vs-crashed handling
(run_cmd cannot express that distinction); its file reads already route through
hostpath (P2.10).

